                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the portfolio`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The spring open-enrollment intake landed <strong>24% below plan</strong>: the flagship face-to-face functional courses filled at <strong>62%</strong> against an 80% break-even, while a global platform teaches the same content at a third of the price, on demand. You had <em>nothing</em> in data/AI or sustainability when three large clients asked. Read the catalog on the <strong>demand-vs-margin</strong> grid: which programs are cash cows, which are structurally declining, where demand is unmet, and where discounting is leaking margin. The first trap is defending the legacy catalog because it is the proven core.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design the pivoted portfolio`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The heart of the pivot. Build next year's catalog by making three coupled choices under the <strong>S/ 4,000,000</strong> budget. <strong>Topics:</strong> launch the emerging-skill programs (AI-for-executives, sustainability, digital leadership) and retire the low-fill seminars — or keep feeding the functional catalog. <strong>Delivery:</strong> match face-to-face, online or blended to topic and audience — the room defends premium value where executives expect it; online scales but walks into the global platforms' price strength. <strong>Capability:</strong> build in-house (slow, owns margin), buy/license (fast, shares margin) or partner (fast, credible, shared economics) for the data-AI and sustainability gaps. Speed to <em>this</em> budget cycle matters.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Win the custom bids and launch`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The two anchor RFPs are live (<strong>S/ 6,000,000</strong> combined, digital leadership + sustainability) and a global provider has just <em>undercut your price</em> on the new online functional programs — while one anchor client now demands <strong>measurable learning outcomes</strong> as a contract condition. Price the custom proposals to clear the <strong>32% margin hurdle</strong> on value, not price; respond to the attack without a margin death-spiral; build outcome measurement into the winning bid; and sequence the launch so scarce senior faculty are not overstretched. Winning the logo is not the same as winning the economics.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover and defend the strategy`,
