                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_grid:          `<strong>That is the portfolio discipline.</strong> Grow the unmet emerging-skill demand, fix what is fixable, retire the structurally declining low-fill seminars. You are reading the catalog as a set of products against demand and margin — not defending tradition. Now make the redesign match the diagnosis.`,
                    coach_r1_protect:       `<strong>“Protect the proven core” is exactly how the core erodes.</strong> That core just filled at 62% and a global platform teaches the same content at a third of the price, on demand. Arce Valencia's point is that a portfolio has to chase where demand is going, not where it has been. What did you plan that a 2026 executive cannot already buy cheaper online?`,
                    coach_r1_retireall:     `<strong>Betting the whole unit on custom throws away real scale.</strong> Open enrollment is squeezed, not dead — it is your reach, your brand top-of-funnel and your NPS engine. A healthy portfolio balances open and custom; retiring almost all of open hands the market to the platforms.`,
                    coach_r1_functional:    `<strong>Reading demand as “still the functional topics” is the structural miss.</strong> Three of your largest clients asked for data/AI and sustainability and you had nothing. Demand has tilted toward emerging skills and flexible delivery. Diagnose where it is going, or you will redesign toward where it has been.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>“It is purely a price game” walks straight into the platforms' strength.</strong> On pure price a global provider beats you every time. Exec-ed defends value through applied, outcome-measured, cohort-based programs — not by becoming the cheapest. Compete where you can win.`,
                    coach_r1_discount:      `<strong>You cut prices 18% to fill the spring courses — fill is back up, congratulations.</strong> But open-program margin just fell below your 32% target and you have trained the market to wait for the discount. You bought enrollment with margin. What does this portfolio look like in two intakes once discounting is the expectation?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You segmented on demand and margin, read demand moving toward emerging skills and flexible delivery, and refused to buy fill with a deep discount. That is the read that lets the Round 2 redesign actually recover margin and fill instead of papering over them.`,
                    coach_r2_emerging:      `<strong>You launched where demand is moving.</strong> AI-for-executives, sustainability and digital leadership are exactly the emerging-skill gaps your clients flagged — and retiring the lowest-fill seminars frees faculty and budget for them. Fill and NPS climb because the catalog finally answers the question executives are actually asking.`,
                    coach_r2_legacy:        `<strong>You refreshed the seminars and launched more of the proven catalog.</strong> But you just rebuilt the portfolio that filled at 62%. A 2026 executive can buy refreshed functional content cheaper online, on demand. Refreshing the legacy is not the same as pivoting toward demand — fill and NPS keep sliding.`,
                    coach_r2_everything:    `<strong>Launching the new topics while keeping every legacy program spreads you thin.</strong> You added emerging-skill demand, good — but carrying the full declining catalog as well dilutes margin and scatters scarce faculty. A pivot retires as deliberately as it launches.`,
                    coach_r2_online:        `<strong>To protect margin you moved the whole catalog online — and NPS fell.</strong> Senior executives paying premium fees expected the room, the network, the cohort. And online is exactly where the global platforms beat you on price. You optimized cost and walked into the competitor's strength. Which programs genuinely needed the room?`,
                    coach_r2_f2f:           `<strong>All face-to-face for prestige caps your scale and clashes with executives' schedules.</strong> The room defends value for some programs — but forcing every topic into Lima classrooms is why fill sits at 62% and why the platforms win on flexibility. Match the mode to the audience, do not impose one.`,
                    coach_r2_build:         `<strong>You decided to build AI-for-executives and sustainability entirely in-house.</strong> Admirable — but in-house build takes three terms and most of your S/ 4M, and your clients issued the RFP for <em>this</em> cycle. By the time you launch, the anchor client has signed with a global provider. When is partnering the right way to be fast to demand, even if you share margin?`,
                    coach_r2_overspend:     `<strong>Spending the whole S/ 4M building everything at once is the over-reach.</strong> You have committed the full budget to the slowest path. There is no reserve for the custom launch or the price attack still coming, and the in-house programs will not land in time. Pace the investment to the demand window.`,
                    coach_r2_underinvest:   `<strong>Holding most of the budget back under-resources the pivot.</strong> A demand-driven realignment needs real investment to launch the new programs and shift delivery. Too cautious and the catalog stays where it was while the budget sits idle. The board gave you S/ 4M to move.`,
                    coach_r3_underprice:    `<strong>You won both RFPs — S/ 6M secured. But you priced them at 24% margin to beat the competitor.</strong> That is below your portfolio target, and now your scarce senior faculty are locked into low-margin custom work instead of higher-margin open programs. Winning the logo is not winning the economics. What did the relationship have to be worth for that margin to make sense?`,
                    coach_r3_premium:       `<strong>Holding a 42% premium protected margin but you only won one bid.</strong> Premium pricing is right when you can defend the value — but priced past what the client will pay, the mandate walks to a competitor. The hurdle is 32%, not the maximum the spreadsheet allows; price to win on value, at or above it.`,
                    coach_r3_match:         `<strong>You matched the global provider's price — a third of yours — to defend volume.</strong> That is the margin death-spiral the post-mortem warned about. You cannot out-cheap a platform with your cost base, and you have just trained the market again. Differentiate on applied content and outcomes, or cede the pure commodity — do not race them to the bottom.`,
                    coach_r3_cede:          `<strong>Ceding the whole online functional space is half-right.</strong> Yielding the pure commodity to the platforms is sensible — but ceding everything online surrenders the flexible delivery executives now want. Cede the commodity; keep the differentiated, applied, cohort-based online and blended programs where you still win.`,
                    coach_r3_promise:       `<strong>Promising outcomes with no measurement behind them is a credibility risk.</strong> The anchor client made measurable outcomes a contract condition. A verbal promise with nothing to measure it reads as a sales line — and when you cannot show the learning ROI, the renewal walks. Build the assessment into the design.`,
                    coach_r3_satisfaction:  `<strong>Satisfaction scores are not what the client asked for.</strong> They demanded measurable <em>learning outcomes</em> — on-the-job impact, not a happy-sheet. Offering satisfaction only signals you cannot deliver applied, outcome-measured programs, which is the whole reason they came to you over a platform.`,
                    coach_r3_customlock:    `<strong>Locking your scarce senior faculty into the custom work stalls the open launches.</strong> The custom mandate matters, but the faculty are a hard cap — pour them all into two contracts and the emerging-skill open programs slip, taking fill and NPS with them. Partner capacity is how you serve both at once.`,
                    coach_r3_bigbang:       `<strong>Launching everything in the same term overstretches the unit.</strong> New open programs and both custom mandates at once exceeds your faculty and partner capacity — quality slips, NPS falls, and you under-deliver on the very programs meant to prove the pivot. Stage it.`,
                    coach_r3_strong:        `<strong>That is the custom play done right.</strong> You priced to clear the margin hurdle on value, differentiated against the price attack instead of matching it, and built outcome measurement into the winning bid. The mandate is won on economics, not just the logo — and the relationship will renew.`,
                    coach_r4_demand:        `<strong>Demand-driven realignment is the philosophy that holds.</strong> Programs are products; you kept the portfolio chasing where employer and learner demand is moving, on margin, with the right mode and partners. That is the story the dean and the board can both believe — and the one the four KPIs back up.`,
                    coach_r4_legacy:        `<strong>“Exec-ed is built on the proven functional catalog” is the philosophy that started the crisis.</strong> The room and the legacy topics have their place, but defending them as the strategy is what let the catalog lag the market. The board will ask why the spring intake fell — and this answer is the cause, not the cure.`,
                    coach_r4_cheapest:      `<strong>“Be the cheapest in every topic” is a war you cannot win.</strong> A global platform has a lower cost base and infinite scale; competing on price hands them the field and bleeds your margin. Exec-ed defends value through applied, outcome-measured, cohort programs — not by under-pricing them.`,
                    coach_r4_speed:         `<strong>That frames the trade-off the way a board trusts.</strong> Build/buy/partner is a speed-margin-differentiation choice: you partnered to reach this cycle's demand before a global provider did, and built in-house where you already had depth. You shared some margin to win the cycle — and named exactly why that was the right call.`,
                    coach_r4_marginonly:    `<strong>“We shared margin because the spreadsheet said so” undersells the decision.</strong> The board's real question is about speed and differentiation, not just a number. Partnering bought you time-to-demand and credibility you could not build in one cycle — make that case, not just the arithmetic.`,
                    coach_r4_apologize:     `<strong>Conceding that partnering was a compromise to unwind concedes the board's premise.</strong> If you frame the partnership as a temporary weakness, you invite them to defund it. Partnering was the right speed-and-differentiation choice for this cycle — own it as strategy, not apology.`,
                    coach_r4_nomonitor:     `<strong>No standing mechanism is how the catalog lagged the market in the first place.</strong> If demand monitoring only happens after enrollment drops, you are always one cycle behind. A quarterly grow/fix/retire loop on employer signals, RFPs, competitor pricing and NPS is what keeps the portfolio ahead of the next pivot.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
